waymo,2026-06,18827345,277829761,275179379,281379379,17091585,21013105,1,1,1,1,1,1,"US est., bottom-up on the Waymo hub's MEASURED Q2 (Sep 24, 2026 update: 271.329M rider-only miles thru Jun-2026; re-chained 2026-09-25): CA = CPUC Q2-2026 driverless actual 9,023,791 deployment + 660,877 pilot (CPUC's total driverless VMT incl. deadhead) x 1.0576, the hub-vs-CPUC ratio for the hub's CA counties in Q2 (Q1: 1.040) = 10.24M (±3%); Maricopa 3.78M and Travis 1.84M = the hub's measured Q2 county increments (11.57M / 5.28M) split by the prior monthly shape (±10%); Atlanta 1.17M = the hub's measured Q2 Fulton+DeKalb increment (3.25M) split by the prior monthly shape (±10%); + rider-only metros the hub does not count (Miami, Dallas, Houston, Orlando, Nashville; San Antonio passenger service halted Apr 20 to Sep 17 after flood strandings Apr 4 and Apr 20) 1.80M [1.05M, 3.00M]. Public freeway rides suspended all month (Waymo's recall filing 26E035000: 'Waymo resumed vehicle operations on freeway June 25, 2026'); Nashville open to all Jun 25; the CPUC pilot file carries the Ojai (Zeekr) trusted-tester rides. The Jun-2026 cumulative lands on the anchor: the hub's 271.329M plus 6.65M estimated in metros the hub does not count, less ~0.15M pre-series miles; its cumulative band is the hub total plus 4.0M to 10.2M for those metros, less 0.15M (data/README.md, Waymo anchors). An Atlanta double-count correction applied 2026-09-25 was withdrawn 2026-09-29: Waymo's corrected per-cell file (CSV4 v2, posted Sep 28, linked from waymo.com/safety/impact since Oct 4-6 (UTC) with release notes dated Oct 2 and uploaded Oct 5, which say the corrected CSV3 and CSV4 'should replace the files released on September 24'; the hub's CSV3 link still serves the Sep 24 file) lists each cell once and sums to the published county totals."
zoox,2026-06,270000,2739920,2511483,3122276,213000,343000,1,1,1,1,1,1,"US est. (rebuilt 2026-10-27): 'nearly two million autonomous miles' (Mar 23-24, 2026) and 'more than 3 million miles on public roads' (Aug 5; Bloomberg, Las Vegas Sun), read as 1.94M and 3.10M, put 1,160,000 miles in Mar 25-Aug 5, 8,656.72 a day. California is CPUC's driverless miles x 1.19 (measured through June, then June's daily rate grown e^0.05 a month); the rest, mostly Las Vegas, 6,180.83 a day, held flat after Aug 5 (no fleet count since ~65 Las Vegas vehicles in service on Sep 2; no pause, recall or halt found). End-July cumulative floor 2,880,483: 'more than 3 million' as of Jul 29-Aug 5, less the pre-series miles' high edge; 'more than' sets no ceiling. Bands: statement rounding, drift from the window's midpoint and month noise (data/README.md, Ghost months; data/README.md, Zoox anchors)."
tesla,2026-07,279000,2719000,2612800,3126000,246000,320000,1,1,1,1,1,1,"Tesla stopped charting miles from every mode after Q2: its Q3-2026 update deck (p8, 'Cumulative Unsupervised Robotaxi Miles') and its Robotaxi Safety Report page (tesla.com/robotaxi/safety, live Oct 21, 2026, which embeds weekly cumulative miles per city) count only driving with no safety monitor in the vehicle, including remote-operator assistance or takeover; Tesla does not say whether repositioning or employee rides are in them. That is a subset of the scope here (every Tesla operator mode, monitor aboard or not), so it sets the floor: July's no-monitor miles are 246,410 on the page (city sums interpolated to Jul 31 within its week) and 251,469 in the deck; floor 246,000. The central adds 33,000 monitor-aboard miles, June's measured remainder (the Q2 deck's 157,000 paid miles less the page's 123,823 no-monitor miles), held flat; the top, 320,000, assumes the monitor-aboard share stayed at or below June's 21% (the higher reading x 1.27). The page's series reproduces Tesla's 'more than 380,000' unsupervised miles of Jul 22 (388,672 by that date). July launches: Miami driverless Jul 3, Tampa and Orlando Jul 21."
