waymo,2026-09,21429027,339495163,323985659,357965047,16578600,27143960,0.1356,0.0615,0.52,0.5952,0.2746,1.0,"US est., bottom-up extension on the hub basis (re-chained 2026-09-25): CA = 10.4M CPUC-basis held (±15%) x the hub-vs-CPUC CA ratio 1.058 [1.040, 1.076] = 11.00M; Maricopa 4.2M (4.3M less ~0.1M for the Phoenix weather pauses below), Travis 1.95M, Atlanta 1.43M (hub Q2 run-rate 1.08M/mo) (±25%); + uncounted metros 2.85M [1.7M, 4.8M] (Denver, San Diego and Tampa riders admitted by invitation from Sep 1 and Las Vegas riders added gradually from Sep 14 (Waymo Help Center; San Diego and Tampa open to anyone from Oct 14, Denver and Las Vegas still by invitation), Las Vegas starting with 'dozens' of Ojai robotaxis (a Waymo spokesperson to TechCrunch, Sep 14), San Diego counted here and not in the CPUC-basis CA term; San Antonio passenger service back Sep 17 for prior-access riders, 'dozens' of cars). Austin freeway rides via Uber from Sep 22 (a Waymo spokesperson to CBS Austin, Sep 23: 'millions of fully autonomous freeway miles we've already driven with riders in Phoenix, Miami, San Francisco, and Los Angeles'); Nashville riders matched on Lyft from Sep 9; Texas AV registrations 736 (Aug 25) to 988 (Sep 2) to 1,133 (Sep 25) to 1,154 (Oct 1; 359 Zeekr Ojai), registered not the same as in service. Cross-check: 21.4M/mo ≈ 5.0M/wk vs Waymo's last stated floor, 'more than 4 million miles every week' (Jun 24). Weather pauses in robotaxitracker app-availability data (every pickup point unavailable): Phoenix Sep 16 13:28 MST to Sep 17 08:30 MST, during NWS flash flood warnings and ending as the last flood warning expired, then 28-30 of 37 points until ~11:20 MST Sep 18, and Sep 29 04:38-07:18 MST (Polo remnants), about 1 demand-weighted day in all (~0.1M, taken off Maricopa); LA Sep 6 17:19-21:09 PDT (flood advisories); Dallas from Sep 30 16:19 CDT, when a flood watch began, into October. No recall after 26E035000 and no new NHTSA investigation through Sep 30. No Waymo total-miles figure measured after Jun 2026 exists; later figures are floors: 'more than 4,000' vehicles (TechCrunch, Sep 1; it already said 'around 4,000' on Jun 29; the Jun 13 recall covered 3,871 5th-gen vehicles) and 'more than half a million paid trips weekly' (Waymo, Sep 15, 2026; the same floor Waymo has stated since late March), while '4M+ mi/wk' was last stated Jun 24. Next anchors: CPUC Q3 (~mid-Nov) and the hub update with data thru Sep (~mid/late Dec)."
